int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
  if (argc != 2) {
    printf("Usage: %s <xml_file>\n", argv[0]);
    return 1;
  }

  char *filename = argv[1];
  char *settings = NULL;

  // Load the XML file into memory
  FILE *fp = fopen(filename, "rb");
  if (!fp) {
    printf("Error opening file %s\n", filename);
    return 1;
  }

  // Use the xmlreader library to parse the XML file
  size_t len = xmlreader_parse(fp, NULL, 0, &settings);
  if (len <= 0) {
    printf("Error parsing XML file %s\n", filename);
    return 1;
  }

  // Clean up
  fclose(fp);

  // Print the settings
  printf("Settings: %s\n", settings);

  free(settings);
  return 0;
}
```
In this example, the program uses the `xmlreader_parse` function from the `xmlreader.h` header to parse the contents of the specified XML file into memory. The parsed data is stored in the `settings` pointer, which is then printed to the console. The `xmlreader_parse` function returns the size of the parsed data in bytes, which is stored in the `len` variable. If the function returns a negative value or `NULL`, an error message is printed and the program exits.

