This line of code is enabling the router middleware in an Express app.

Express is a popular web framework for Node.js. The app object represents an Express application.

The app.router is the built-in router middleware for Express. By calling app.use(app.router), you are mounting the router middleware onto the app.

The router middleware is responsible for dispatching routes that match the URL of incoming requests to the appropriate route handlers.

So in short, this line is saying "use the built-in router middleware for this Express app".

Without this line, any route handlers you define would not work, because the requests would not get routed to them.

For example:
```js
const express = require('express')
const app = express()

// Enable the router 
app.use(app.router) 

// Define a route
app.get('/about', (req, res) => {
  res.send('About page')
})

app.listen(3000)
```

Here the app.use(app.router) line enables the router, which then allows the /about route we defined to work.

