In C, a global variable is defined outside of any function, and its storage is allocated in the data segment of the program.

By default, a global variable has a "static" storage class specifier, which means that it retains its value between function calls. However, if a global variable is defined with the "register" storage class specifier, it will be treated as a register variable, and its value will be lost between function calls.

Here's an example of defining a global variable with the "static" storage class specifier:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

void myFunction() {
    int globalVar = 42;
    printf("The value of globalVar is %d.\n", globalVar);
}

int main() {
    myFunction();
    printf("The value of globalVar is %d.\n", globalVar);
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the variable `globalVar` is defined outside of any function, and its value is initialized to 42. When `myFunction()` is called, `globalVar` is used to print its value to the console. When `main()` is called again, `globalVar` is accessed again to print its value to the console.

If we had defined `globalVar` with the `register` storage class specifier, like this:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

void myFunction() {
    register int globalVar = 42;
    printf("The value of globalVar is %d.\n", globalVar);
}

int main() {
    myFunction();
    printf("The value of globalVar is %d.\n", globalVar);
    return 0;
}
```
Then `globalVar` would be treated as a register variable, and its value would be lost between function calls. Instead, `main()` would see a new copy of the variable with a new value of 42 each time it is called.

