The callback parameter is meant to be a function that is called when the query completes. It should have the signature:

```js
(err, results) => {
  // ...
}
```

So your code should have a function defined and passed as the callback, like this:

```js
const callback = (err, results) => {
  if (err) throw err
  else console.log("Successful GET!")
}

connection.query(query, [email], callback) 
```

Then when the query is done, the callback function will be executed, passing the error (if any) and the results.

In your code, callback is probably undefined, hence the TypeError when you try to call it.

To fix this, you just need to define and pass an actual function as the callback, like in the example above.

