Certainly! CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a security feature implemented by web browsers to prevent web pages from making requests to a different origin (domain, protocol, or port) than the one the web page was served from. This can cause issues when trying to consume RESTful APIs from a Spring Backend, especially if the API and the web application are hosted on different domains or ports.

To solve CORS issues in a Spring Backend, you can use the `@CrossOrigin` annotation on the REST controller or the endpoint method. Here are some possible solutions:

1. Use the `@CrossOrigin` annotation on the REST controller:
```java
@RestController
@CrossOrigin(origins = "http://example.com")
public class MyRestController {
    // REST endpoint methods go here
}
```
This annotation allows requests from the specified origin (`http://example.com` in this case). You can replace `http://example.com` with any domain or URL that you want to allow.

2. Use the `@CrossOrigin` annotation on the endpoint method:
```java
@RestController
public class MyRestController {
    @CrossOrigin(origins = "http://example.com")
    @GetMapping("/api/data")
    public ResponseEntity<String> getData() {
        // endpoint implementation
    }
}
```
This annotation allows requests from the specified origin only on the `/api/data` endpoint.

3. Use the `HttpSecurity` class to configure CORS:
```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebMvc
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {
    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.cors().and()
                .allowedOrigins("http://example.com")
                .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE")
                .allowCredentials(true)
                .maxAge(3600);
    }
}
```
This configuration allows requests from the specified origin (`http://example.com`) with the specified methods (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) and enables CORS for the entire application.

