To make async calls to OpenAPI-generated APIs in a Java Spring Boot application, you can use the `` @RestController `` and `` @ResponseBody `` annotations to define your controller and response, respectively, and use the `` @Autowired `` annotation to inject an instance of the `` AsyncHttpClient `` class from the `` org.springframework.http.client.AsyncHttpClient `` interface.

Here's an example of how you can use the `` AsyncHttpClient `` to make an async call to an OpenAPI-generated API and return a response:


```java
@RestController
public class ApiController {

    @Autowired
    private AsyncHttpClient asyncHttpClient;

    @GetMapping("/example")
    public ResponseEntity<NAME_1> example(@RequestParam NAME_1 param) {
        AsyncHttpResponse response = asyncHttpClient.post(
            "https://example.com/api",
            RequestEntity.builder()
                .body(param)
                .build()
        );
        return new ResponseEntity<>(response.getBody(), response.getStatusCode());
    }
}

```

In this example, the `` @RestController `` annotation indicates that this class is a controller, and the `` @GetMapping `` annotation indicates that this is a GET request to the `` /example `` endpoint. The `` @ResponseBody `` annotation indicates that the response will be a `` ResponseEntity `` object, which encapsulates both the response status code and the response body.

The `` @Autowired `` annotation is used to inject an instance of the `` AsyncHttpClient `` class from the Spring container. This makes it easy to manage the lifecycle of the `` AsyncHttpClient `` and ensure that it's properly configured and destroyed.

To handle the response from the API, the `` example `` method uses the `` AsyncHttpResponse `` class, which is a subclass of `` ResponseEntity ``, and returns a `` ResponseEntity `` object with the response status code and the response body.

Note that this example assumes that you have already obtained an access token and configured the `` AsyncHttpClient `` to use the access token. You can find more information about how to do this in the Spring documentation for the `` AsyncHttpClient `` class.

