using System.Threading.Tasks;

// Assuming inventoryItems is a list of strings representing item identifiers
foreach (var inventoryItem in inventoryItems.OrderBy(i => i))
{
    // Query the database for the item and subtract its current count from the list
    var itemInDatabase = inventoryItems.FirstOrDefault(i => i.Equals(inventoryItem));
    if (itemInDatabase != null)
    {
        var currentCount = itemInDatabase.CurrentCount;
        // Subtract the current count from the list
        inventoryItems.Where(i => i.Equals(itemInDatabase.Identifier)).ToList().ForEach(i => i.CurrentCount -= currentCount);
    }
}

// Wrap the database access code in a transaction
var transaction = Parallel.Transaction(() =>
{
    foreach (var inventoryItem in inventoryItems.OrderBy(i => i))
    {
        // Query the database for the item and subtract its current count from the list
        var itemInDatabase = inventoryItems.FirstOrDefault(i => i.Equals(inventoryItem));
        if (itemInDatabase != null)
        {
            var currentCount = itemInDatabase.CurrentCount;
            // Subtract the current count from the list
            inventoryItems.Where(i => i.Equals(itemInDatabase.Identifier)).ToList().ForEach(i => i.CurrentCount -= currentCount);
        }
    }
});
```
In this example, we use the `OrderBy` method to sort the inventory items before processing them in a parallel foreach loop. We then wrap the database access code in a `Parallel.Transaction` method to ensure that the database operations are executed atomically.

Note that the `Parallel.Transaction` method is only supported in .NET 6 and later versions, so if you're using an earlier version of .NET, you'll need to use a different approach to achieve transactional behavior.

