1. First, you will need to include the necessary libraries for reading and writing to a flat file. For example, in C++ you could use the `fstream` library for reading and writing to a file stream, and the `iostream` library for input/output streams.
```c++
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
```
2. Next, you will need to create a new configuration class that will be responsible for reading and writing the configuration values to the flat file. This class should have member functions for reading and writing individual configuration values, as well as for reading and writing the entire configuration.
```c++
class Config {
private:
    std::string fileName;
    std::map<std::string, std::string> configData;

public:
    Config(const std::string& fileName) : fileName(fileName) {}

    void readConfig() {
        std::ifstream file(fileName);
        std::string line;
        while (std::getline(file, line)) {
            size_t pos = line.find_first_of(" \t\n");
            if (pos != std::string::npos) {
                std::string key = line.substr(0, pos);
                std::string value = line.substr(pos + 1);
                configData[key] = value;
            }
        }
    }

    void writeConfig() {
        std::ofstream file(fileName);
        for (const auto& [key, value] : configData) {
            file << key << " " << value << std::endl;
        }
    }

    std::string get(const std::string& key) const {
        return configData[key];
    }
};
```
3. In the main function of your application, you can create an instance of the configuration class and use its member functions to read and write the configuration values.
```c++
