```rust
struct User {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
}

fn main() {
    let mut user = User { name: String::from("NAME_1"), age: 30 };

    // The lifetime of `user` is inferred as `'static`
    // because it is defined outside of any function
    let name = user.name;

    // The lifetime of `user` is inferred as `'static`
    // because it is referenced from within the same function
    println!("{}", user.age);

    // The lifetime of `user` is inferred as `'static`
    // because it is referenced from within the same function
    println!("{}", name);

    // The lifetime of `user` is inferred as `'static`
    // because it is referenced from within the same function
    println!("{}", user.name);

    // The lifetime of `user` is inferred as `'static`
    // because it is referenced from within the same function
    println!("{}", user.age);
}
```
In this example, the `User` struct has two fields: `name` and `age`. The `main` function creates a new `User` instance and assigns values to its fields. The `name` and `age` fields are then used in the `println!` macro calls, which print their values to the console.

The Rust compiler infers the lifetime of the `user` variable as `'static`, which means that it has the same lifetime as the variable itself. This is because `user` is defined outside of any function and is referenced from within the same function. The same applies to the `name` and `user` variables, which are referenced from within the same function and have the same lifetime as `user`.

By defining the lifetime of variables and references, Rust ensures that references are always valid and that memory is freed when it is no longer needed, which helps prevent memory-related errors.

