Hooks are a new addition in React 16.8. They let you use state and other React features without writing a class component.

Some of the main hooks are:

- useState: Lets you add state to functional components.

```jsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

<p>You clicked {count} times</p>
<button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
  Click me
</button>
```

- useEffect: Lets you perform side effects in functional components.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
});
```

- useContext: Lets you use React context in functional components.

```jsx
const MyContext = React.createContext();

<MyContext.Provider value={/* some value */}>

function Child() {
  const value = useContext(MyContext);
  /* use value */  
}  
```  

- useRef: Lets you access DOM nodes or React elements created in the render method.

```jsx 
const ref = useRef(null);

<input ref={ref} />

// Access DOM node:
const input = ref.current;  
```

- useMemo: Memoizes a value and returns it on subsequent renders unless one of the dependencies has changed.

```jsx
const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b]);
```  

- useCallback: Memoizes a callback function and returns it on subsequent renders unless one of the dependencies has changed.

```jsx
const memoizedCallback = useCallback(
  () => doSomething(a, b), 
  [a, b]
);
```

Hooks let you reuse stateful logic between components, making it easy to extract duplicated logic between components.

