Here is the code modified to use connection.query instead of db.get:

```js
passport.use(new LocalStrategy(function verify(username_email, password, cb) {
  const query = 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? OR email = ?';
  connection.query(query, [ username_email ], function(err, results) {
    if (err) { return cb(err); }
    if (!results.length) { return cb(null, false, { message: 'Incorrect username or email.' }); }
    const user = results[0];

    bcrypt.hash(password, 10, function(err, hashedPassword) {
      if (err) { return cb(err); } 

      bcrypt.compare(hashedPassword, user.password, function(err, result) {
        if (err) { return cb(err); }
        if (!result) {
          return cb(null, false, { message: 'Incorrect password.' });
        }
        return cb(null, user);
      });
    });
  });
}));
```

The main changes are:

1. connection.query is used to execute a query and get results, instead of db.get.

2. The results are an array, so we check results.length instead of !user.

3. We grab the first (and only) user from results and store in the user variable.

4. The user variable is then used in place of results[0] for the rest of the logic.

Everything else remains the same. connection.query allows executing a generic query, whereas db.get implies fetching a single result by ID. So this change makes the code a bit more robust and flexible.

