Here is a C function that encodes a signed 32-bit integer as a sequence of LEB128 encoded integers:

```c
void encode_s32_leb128(int32_t value) {
  uint32_t uint_value;
  if (value < 0) {
    uint_value = ~(uint32_t)value + 1;
  } else {
    uint_value = value;
  }

  do {
    uint8_t byte = uint_value & 0x7f;
    uint_value >>= 7;
    if (uint_value) byte |= 0x80;
    putchar(byte);
  } while (uint_value);
}
```

It works as follows:

- If the input value is negative, we take its two's complement to get the unsigned equivalent 
- We then encode the unsigned value using LEB128:
  - We mask out the lowest 7 bits and emit that as a byte
  - We shift the value right by 7 bits
  - If the value is non-zero, we set the MSB of the byte to 1, indicating more bytes follow
  - We emit the byte 
- We repeat this process until the value is zero, indicating the end of the LEB128 encoding

