You can change scenario object status in JUnit5 by using the `@Before` and `@After` annotations.

Here's an example:
```java
import org.junit.Before;
import org.junit.After;
import org.junit.Test;

public class MyTest {

    @Before
    public void setup() {
        // Change the status of the scenario object here
        scenarioObject.setStatus("failed");
    }

    @After
    public void tearDown() {
        // Change the status of the scenario object here
        scenarioObject.setStatus("completed");
    }

    @Test
    public void testMyMethod() {
        // Run the scenario here
        scenarioObject.run();
    }
}
```

In the `setup()` method, you can change the status of the scenario object by setting its `status` property. For example, if you want the scenario to fail, you can set its `status` property to `"failed"`.

In the `tearDown()` method, you can change the status of the scenario object by setting its `status` property. For example, if you want the scenario to be completed, you can set its `status` property to `"completed"`.

In the `testMyMethod()` method, you can run the scenario as normal, but you can also check its status by accessing the `scenarioObject.getStatus()` method.

Note that you can also use the `@BeforeClass` and `@AfterClass` annotations to run the same setup and tearDown methods before and after all tests in the class, respectively.

