Yes, you can use the `std::is_base_of` template to detect whether the base class is the same as the current class. In this case, if the base class is the same as the current class, then it's a regular member function, otherwise, it's a template function. Here's an example:
```cpp
#include <type_traits>

struct S {
    void f(int) {}
    template <class T>
    void f(T) {}
};

template <typename T>
bool is_regular_member_function(T *obj, std::remove_const_t<T> *member_name) {
    return std::is_base_of<T, S>::value &&
           *obj == *dynamic_cast<S*>(obj) &&
           member_name == typeid(S::f).name();
}

int main() {
    S s;
    (s.*(s.f))(42); // calls regular member function
    (s.*(S::f))(42); // calls template function

    bool is_regular_member_function_result = is_regular_member_function(&s, typeid(S::f).name());
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << is_regular_member_function_result << std::endl; // prints false

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we define a template function `is_regular_member_function` that takes a pointer to the object and the name of the member function as input parameters. It checks if the base class is the same as the current class and if the member name is the same as the member function name. If both conditions are true, it returns true, indicating that the function is a regular member function, otherwise, it returns false.

In the main function, we create an object of the `S` struct and call its member functions. We then use the `is_regular_member_function` template to check if the `f` function passed to it is a regular member function or a template function.

Note that this solution assumes that the member function name is available at compile-time, which may not always be the case in more complex scenarios.

