Yes, you can partition an array of two types of coins (x and y) in linear time using a technique called "divide and conquer."

Here's one possible implementation in JavaScript:
```javascript
function partitionCoins(coins) {
  // If there's only one coin type in the array, return a singleton array containing that coin.
  if (coins.length === 1) {
    return [coins[0]];
  }

  // Choose a pivot coin that will be in its own partition.
  // You can choose any coin, but using the last coin in the array as the pivot is a good choice.
  const pivot = coins[coins.length - 1];

  // Partition the coins into two arrays: "x" coins on the left of the pivot and "y" coins on the right.
  const xCoins = [];
  const yCoins = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < coins.length; i++) {
    const coin = coins[i];
    if (coin === pivot) {
      xCoins.push(coin);
    } else if (coin > pivot) {
      yCoins.push(coin);
    } else {
      xCoins.push(coin);
    }
  }

  // Combine the x and y coins into two arrays.
  const result = [xCoins, yCoins];
  return result;
}
```
This implementation first checks if the array has only one coin type, in which case it returns a singleton array containing that coin. Otherwise, it chooses a pivot coin and partitions the other coins into two arrays based on whether they are less than or greater than the pivot coin. Finally, it combines the two partitions into two separate arrays and returns them as the result.

Note that the time complexity of this implementation is O(n), where n is the length of the input array, because the partition step takes linear time O(n) and the combine step is constant time O(1). Therefore, the overall time complexity of the partition function is O(n).

