If you want to remove an event listener from a node in JavaScript, you can do so using the `removeEventListener()` method. This method requires you to pass in the event name and the function that was originally bound to the event.

Here's an example:

```javascript
const myFunction = function() {
  console.log('Hello, world!');
};

const myNode = document.querySelector('my-node');

// Add event listener
myNode.addEventListener('click', myFunction);

// Remove event listener
myNode.removeEventListener('click', myFunction);
```

In this example, `myFunction` is removed as a listener for the 'click' event.

Remember, when removing event listeners, you must pass the exact function reference that was used when the event listener was added. If you pass a different function, even if it's identical to the original, it won't work because it's a different reference.

If you don't have a reference to the original function, you cannot use `removeEventListener()` to remove it. This is a common issue with anonymous functions, as they don't have a reference that can be used later.

```javascript
// Add event listener
myNode.addEventListener('click', function() {
  console.log('Hello, world!');
});

// This won't work
myNode.removeEventListener('click', function() {
  console.log('Hello, world!');
});
```

In this case, the two anonymous functions are not the same reference, so the `removeEventListener()` call doesn't remove the event listener.

For the `getEventListeners()` API, you can use it in Chrome devtools to inspect event listeners on an element, but you cannot use it to remove the event listeners.

