```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int nums[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int i;
    printf("The binary representation of the numbers in the array is: ");
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("%d ", nums[i]);
        if (nums[i] > 0) {
            printf("1");
        } else {
            printf("0");
        }
        printf(" ");
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```
In this program, we define an array of integers `nums` containing the values 10, 20, 30, 40, and 50. We then use a `for` loop to iterate through the array and print each element in binary format. If the value of the element is greater than 0, we print "1", otherwise we print "0". We start the loop with an offset of 0, so the first element of the array is printed as "10" (the value of the first element), the second element is printed as "101" (the value of the second element in binary), and so on. The last element of the array is printed as "11011" (the value of the last element in binary). The binary representation of the numbers in the array is then printed at the end of the loop.

