No, it is not safe to std::move a structure created on the stack.

When you create an object on the stack, it is destroyed when the stack frame is popped. So if you std::move from that object, the moved-from object is left in an undefined state, and then immediately destroyed. This can lead to undefined behavior.

For example:
```cpp
struct NAME_1 {
    NAME_1() { std::cout << "Constructed\n"; }
    ~NAME_1() { std::cout << "Destructed\n"; }
};

void bar() {
    NAME_1 f; // Created on stack
    NAME_1 g = std::move(f); // Moves from f, leaving f in undefined state

    // f is destroyed here, potentially leading to undefined behavior
}
```

This would be undefined behavior, because f is destroyed after being left in an undefined state.

To fix this, you should create the object on the heap instead, and properly delete it after the move:

```cpp
void bar() {
    NAME_1* f = new NAME_1(); 
    NAME_1 g = std::move(*f); 
    delete f; // Now safe to destroy f
}
```

Now f is deleted after the move, so it is safe.

In summary, it is not safe to std::move from stack objects, since their lifetime ends immediately after the move. You should create heap objects instead, and manage their lifetime properly around moves.

