To achieve interpolation, you can use the [SyncVar] attribute to synchronize the value of a variable between the server and clients. By default, Mirror updates the value of the SyncVar on clients using the "snap" method, which means the value is updated instantly without any interpolation.

However, if you want to perform interpolation on clients, you can use the [SyncVar(hook = "InterpolatePosition")] attribute. This attribute allows you to specify a custom method that will be called whenever the value of the SyncVar changes on clients.

In the InterpolatePosition method, you can implement interpolation logic to smoothly update the position of the object on clients. You can use techniques such as lerping or slerping to interpolate between the old and new positions over time.

Here's an example of how you can perform interpolation on clients using SyncVars in Mirror:

```csharp
using Mirror;
using UnityEngine;

public class Player : NetworkBehaviour
{
    [SyncVar(hook = "InterpolatePosition")]
    private Vector3 syncPosition;

    private float lerpRate = 15f;

    private void InterpolatePosition(Vector3 newPosition)
    {
        // Perform interpolation between old and new position
        StartCoroutine(InterpolatePositionCoroutine(newPosition));
    }

    private IEnumerator InterpolatePositionCoroutine(Vector3 newPosition)
    {
        float elapsedTime = 0f;
        Vector3 startPosition = transform.position;

        while (elapsedTime < lerpRate)
        {
            elapsedTime += Time.deltaTime;
            float t = elapsedTime / lerpRate;
            transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPosition, newPosition, t);

            yield return null;
        }

        transform.position = newPosition;
    }
}
```

In this example, the syncPosition variable is a SyncVar that represents the position of the player. Whenever the value of syncPosition changes on clients, the InterpolatePosition method is called. Inside this method, we start a coroutine InterpolatePositionCoroutine that performs the interpolation over time.

Note that this is just a basic example, and you may need to modify it based on your specific requirements and the type of object you are interpolating.

