Good point, the code above only saves the main HTML, it doesn't include iframe contents.

To save iframe contents as well, we'll need to execute a script in each iframe to get its HTML. We can do that like this:

background.js:
```js
chrome.browserAction.onClicked.addListener(function(tab) {
  chrome.tabs.executeScript(tab.id, {
    code: 'document.documentElement.outerHTML'  
  }, function(html) {
    chrome.storage.local.set({mainHtml: html[0]});

    // Get all iframes 
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(tab.id, {
      code: 'document.querySelectorAll("iframe")'
    }, function(frames) {

      // Execute script in each iframe to get its HTML
      frames.forEach(function(frame) {
        chrome.tabs.executeScript(tab.id,{
          frameId: frame.frameId,  
          code: 'document.documentElement.outerHTML'
        }, function(iframeHtml) {
          chrome.storage.local.set({
            [frame.src]: iframeHtml[0]  
          });
        });
      });
    });      
  });
});
```

This will:

- Save the main HTML in `mainHtml` 
- Get all iframes 
- For each iframe:
  - Execute a script in that iframe to get its HTML
  - Save the iframe HTML keyed by its `src` attribute

So your storage will end up with:

```json
{
  "mainHtml": "<html>...</html>",
  "iframe1.com": "<html>...</html>",
