Good catch! This approach will likely run into cross-origin issues when trying to access iframes from a different domain.

Chrome's Content Security Policy (CSP) prevents scripts from accessing iframes from a different origin for security reasons. So this approach will likely only work for iframes on the same origin/domain as the main page.

To access cross-origin iframes, we would need:

1. Relax the CSP of the iframe, by adding a `frame-ancestors` directive in its `<head>` allowing the main page's domain.
2. Use postMessage() to communicate between the main page and the iframe, instead of directly accessing its DOM.

For example:

main.html:
```html
<iframe src="http://different-domain.com"></iframe>
```

iframe.html:
```html
<head>
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="frame-ancestors 'self' http://main-domain.com">
</head>
<body>...</body>
```

background.js:
```js
// Post message to iframe asking for its HTML
iframe.contentWindow.postMessage({ getHtml: true }, '*');

// Listen for message back with HTML    
window.addEventListener('message', function(event) {
  if (event.origin === iframe.src) {
    chrome.storage.local.set({
      [iframe.src]: event.data.html
    });   
  }
})
```

iframe.js:
```js
// Listen for postMessage 
window.addEventListener('message', function() {
  // Send HTML back via postMessage
  window.parent.postMessage({ html: document.documentElement.outerHTML }, '*');
})
```

This approach uses postMessage() to communicate between domains, which is allowed by the CSP.

