Here's an example program in C to create a child process using the `fork` system call:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;
    int status;

    pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0) {
        printf("Child process:\n");
        execl("cat", "cat", (char *)NULL);
    } else {
        printf("Parent process:\n");
        waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this program, the `fork` function creates a new process, which is a copy of the parent process. The return value of `fork` is the process ID of the child process, which is stored in the variable `pid`.

The child process executes the `execl` function to replace its current process image with the `cat` program. The `execl` function takes three arguments: the name of the program, the name of the program's entry point, and an array of command line arguments.

In the parent process, the `waitpid` function is used to wait for the child process to complete. The return value of `waitpid` is the exit status of the child process, which indicates whether the child process completed successfully or encountered an error.

The output of the program will depend on whether the `cat` program is installed on the system and what it prints to the console.

