There are a few key advantages of using console.assert() over console.log():

1. console.assert() will only print the message if the assertion fails. If the assertion passes, it will not print anything. This keeps the console clean and only prints messages when there are issues.

2. console.assert() clearly indicates an assertion failure, making it easy to spot issues. The message is preceded by "Assertion failed: ".

3. console.assert() allows you to specify a second argument which is a boolean. The message will only print if that boolean is falsy. This allows you to easily enable/disable certain assertions.

4. In non-development builds, console.assert() statements are automatically removed. This means they will not impact performance in production. console.log() statements will still run in production.

For example:
```js
// Prints
console.log('User logged in'); 

// Does not print
console.assert(true, 'User logged in');

// Prints "Assertion failed: User not logged in"
console.assert(false, 'User logged in');

// Will not print in production
console.assert(false, 'Development check'); 
```

So in summary, console.assert() gives you cleaner, more purposeful logging that is optimized for development and production. I would recommend using console.assert() for any internal validation checks and only using console.log() for general debugging logs.

