```kotlin
const readLines = (fd, position, numLines) => {
  const lines = [];
  let endPosition = 0;
  let buffer = "";

  while (endPosition < numLines) {
    const bytesRead = process.stdout.read(fd, buffer, endPosition);
    if (bytesRead === -1) {
      break;
    }
    buffer += process.stdout.read(fd, buffer, endPosition);
    endPosition += bytesRead;
    while (buffer.length > 0 && buffer.charCodeAt(0) === 10) {
      const newlineIndex = buffer.indexOf("\n", 0);
      if (newlineIndex === -1) {
        break;
      }
      lines.push(buffer.substring(0, newlineIndex).replace(/\r?\n/, ""));
      buffer = buffer.substring(newlineIndex + 1);
      endPosition += newlineIndex + 1;
    }
  }
  return [lines, endPosition];
};

const countLines = (fd) => {
  const [lines, endPosition] = readLines(fd, 0, 100000); // Read up to 100000 lines
  const numLines = Math.ceil(endPosition / (1024 * 1024)); // Calculate the number of lines based on the end position
  return numLines;
};
```
The `countLines` function simply calls the `readLines` function to read up to 100000 lines of the file, and then calculates the total number of lines based on the end position of the last line read. Note that this implementation assumes that each line is represented by a single byte of file data, which may not always be the case (e.g. if the file uses UTF-8 encoding).

