This function takes an `Image` object as input and returns a byte array containing the image's pixels. It first creates a new `Bitmap` object from the input image and then saves the bitmap to a `MemoryStream` as a JPEG image. Finally, it returns the byte array containing the image's data.

Here's an updated version of the function that automatically determines the image format:
```csharp
using System.IO;
using System.Drawing;

public static byte[] ConvertImageToByteArray(Image image)
{
    // Get the image's pixels
    Bitmap bitmap = new Bitmap(image);
    byte[] imageBytes;

    // Determine the image format
    string fileExtension = Path.GetExtension(image.FileName);

    // Save the image to a MemoryStream in the appropriate format
    if (fileExtension == ".jpg")
    {
        bitmap.Save(image.OutputStream, ImageFormat.Jpeg);
    }
    else if (fileExtension == ".png")
    {
        bitmap.Save(image.OutputStream, ImageFormat.Png);
    }
    else if (fileExtension == ".bmp")
    {
        bitmap.Save(image.OutputStream, ImageFormat.Bmp);
    }
    else
    {
        // Return an empty byte array if the format is not supported
        return new byte[0];
    }

    // Convert the image to a byte array
    imageBytes = new byte[bitmap.Width * bitmap.Height * 3];
    bitmap.GetData(imageBytes);

    return imageBytes;
}
```
This version of the function first determines the file extension of the input image and then saves the image to a `MemoryStream` in the appropriate format (JPEG, PNG, or BMP). It then extracts the image's data from the `MemoryStream` and returns a byte array containing the image's pixels.

