This statement is true. In PHP, variables within the scope of a function lose their values once the function finishes executing.

PHP has function scope - variables declared within a function only exist within that function. Once the function ends, those variables are destroyed. 

For example:
```php
function myFunction() {
  $x = 1;
}

echo $x; // Will output nothing, $x is not defined outside the function
```

The $x variable only exists within myFunction(), so trying to access it outside the function results in an undefined variable error.

This happens because PHP allocates memory for variables when a function starts, and deallocates that memory when the function ends. So the variable values are lost.

To preserve variable values outside of a function, you need to declare them as global, static, or return them from the function:

```php
$x = 1;  // Global variable

function myFunction() {
  global $x;
  $x = 2; 
}

echo $x; // Outputs 2, $x is global

function myFunction() {
  static $x = 1;
  $x++; 
}

echo $x; // Outputs 2, $x is static

function myFunction() {
  $x = 1;
  return $x; 
} 

$result = myFunction();
echo $result; // Outputs 1, return value is preserved
```

So in summary, yes - variables within the scope of a PHP function lose their values once the function finishes executing, unless they are global, static or returned from the function.

