fread is a C library function that is used to read a specified number of bytes from a file-like stream into a character array. It is typically used to read input from a file, such as a text file or a console, into a buffer for processing.

The function takes three arguments:

*   read\_ptr: a pointer to a character array that will hold the data read from the file.
*   buffer\_size: the size of the character array, in bytes.
*   num\_chars: the number of characters to read.

fread reads up to buffer\_size bytes of data from the file, starting at the current position of the file, and stores them in read\_ptr. It returns the number of characters read, which may be less than the requested number if there is not enough data available at the current position.

Here's an example of how to use fread to read data from a file into a buffer:


```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    FILE *fp;
    char buffer[100];
    int num_chars;

    fp = fopen("input.txt", "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Error: Could not open input file\n");
        return 1;
    }

    while (fread(&num_chars, sizeof(num_chars), 1, fp) != 1) {
        printf("Error: Could not read from file\n");
        break;
    }

    if (num_chars == 0) {
        printf("Error: No characters read from file\n");
        return 1;
    }

    fread(buffer, num_chars, 1, fp);

    printf("Read %d characters from file\n", num_chars);
    printf("Read data: %s\n", buffer);

    fclose(fp);

    return 0;
}

```

