```javascript
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Map();

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    if (visited.has(node)) {
      continue;
    }
    visited.set(node, true);

    // Visit all the neighbors of the current node
    const neighbors = graph[node];
    for (const neighbor of neighbors) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This implementation uses a queue to keep track of the nodes to visit and a map to keep track of the visited nodes. The algorithm starts by adding the starting node to the queue and marking it as visited. Then, it iterates through the queue, for each node, it visits all the neighbors of the current node and adds them to the queue if they are not visited before. The algorithm stops when the queue is empty.

You can use this function like this:
```javascript
