There are a few ways to reflect an API failure in the UI from Vuex:

1. Set an error message in the state. For example:

```js
// In a Vuex action
apiCall() {
  api.request()
    .catch(err => {
      this.state.error = 'API failed'
    })
}

// In your component
error: this.$store.state.error 
```

Then in your template you can show the error message:

```html
<p v-if="error">{{ error }}</p>
```

2. Set a loading boolean to false in the state. For example:

```js
// In a Vuex action
apiCall() {
  this.state.loading = true
  api.request()
    .catch(err => {
      this.state.loading = false
    })
}

// In your component
loading: this.$store.state.loading
```

Then in your template you can hide a loading indicator:

```html
<loading-spinner v-if="loading"></loading-spinner>
```

3. Dispatch an error event that components can listen for. For example:

```js
// In a Vuex action
apiCall() {
  api.request()
    .catch(err => {
      this.$emit('error', err.message)
    })
}

// In your component
created() {
  this.$store.on('error', (message) => {
    this.error = message
  })
}
```

Then in your template:

```html
<p v-if="error">{{ error }}</p>
```

