```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define TRUE 1
#define FALSE 0

// Function to perform bogosort on an array of items
// The function returns a sorted version of the array
void bogosort(int items[], int n) {
    // The size of the items array
    unsigned int i;

    // Initialize the swap list
    int *swaps = (int *) malloc(sizeof(int) * n);
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        swaps[i] = i;
    }

    // NAME_1 until the sort is stabilized
    while (TRUE) {
        // Choose a random element from the swap list
        int choice = rand() % n;

        // If the choice is not None, swap the element with the last unsorted element
        if (swaps[choice] != -1) {
            int last_unsorted_index = n - 1;
            swaps[choice] = items[last_unsorted_index];
            items[last_unsorted_index] = -1;
        }

        // Check if the sort is stabilized
        bool stabilized = TRUE;
        for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (items[i] == -1) {
                stabilized = FALSE;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (stabilized) {
            break;
        }
    }

    // Copy the sorted items array from the swap list
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        items[i] = swaps[i];
    }
}

// Example usage
int main() {
    int items[] = {3, 1, 2, 4, 5};
    int n = sizeof(items) / sizeof(items[0]);

    // Sort the array using bogosort
    bogosort(items, n);

    // Print the sorted array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", items[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation of bogosort is similar to the Python implementation, but it is written in C and uses standard library functions such as `rand` for generating random numbers, `malloc` for dynamic memory allocation, and `TRUE` and `FALSE` for boolean values. The function takes an array of items as input and returns a sorted version of the array. The algorithm works by repeatedly swapping the last unsorted element with a randomly chosen element from the swap list. The swap list is initialized with `0` values for each element in the input array, which are used to mark the last unsorted element. The algorithm continues until the sort is stabilized, which means that all elements are either in their final sorted position or have been swapped with another element that is also in its final sorted position.

Note that this implementation is less efficient than some other sorting algorithms, but it can still be useful for sorting small lists or for educational purposes.

