If you don't want to statically link the shared library, you can use the `dlopen` and `dlsym` functions from the `runtime` package to dynamically load and call functions from the shared library. Here's an example:
```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "runtime"
    "syscall"
)

func main() {
    // Load the shared library
    libraryPath := "/path/to/shared/library.so"
    handle := runtime.Dlopen(libraryPath, syscall.RTLD_NOW)
    if handle == nil {
        fmt.Printf("Failed to load library: %s\n", libraryPath)
        return
    }

    // Get a pointer to the function you want to call
    functionName := "function_name"
    function := runtime.Dlsym(handle, functionName)
    if function == nil {
        fmt.Printf("Failed to find function: %s\n", functionName)
        return
    }

    // Call the function
    result := function(arg1, arg2, arg3)
    fmt.Printf("Function returned: %d\n", result)

    // Close the library handle
    runtime.Dlclose(handle)
}
```
Note that you will need to use the correct function name when calling `dlsym`, as well as pass the correct number and types of arguments to the function. Also, make sure that the shared library is installed in the correct location on your system.

