Spring Cache annotation allows you to easily add caching to your methods. The main annotations are:

- @Cacheable: Triggers cache population. The method return value is cached according to the specified cache name and key.
- @CacheEvict: Triggers cache eviction. The cached data is removed from the cache.
- @CachePut: Updates the cache without interfering with the method execution. 
- @Caching: Allows grouping multiple cache operations.
- @CacheConfig: Shares some common cache-related settings at class-level.

For example:
```java
@Cacheable("books")
public NAME_1 findBook(Integer id) {
    // ...
}

@CacheEvict("books")
public void deleteBook(Integer id) {
    // ...
}

@CachePut("books") 
public NAME_1 updateBook(NAME_1 NAME_1) {
    // ...
}
```

This will cache the findBook() method according to the "books" cache. The deleteBook() method will evict the cache for that NAME_1. And the updateBook() method will update the cache with the new NAME_1 data.

Some other things you can configure are:

- Cache key: Using the key attribute. You can specify a SpEL expression to generate the key dynamically.
- Cache manager: The cache manager to use. By default it uses a ConcurrentMapCacheManager. 
- Cache resolver: To resolve runtime cache names.
- Cache condition: A SpEL expression that determines if the method should be cached or not.
- And more...

So the Spring Cache abstraction allows you to easily add caching to your application with minimal effort.

Here's a full example of using @Cacheable:

```java
@Cacheable("books")
public NAME_1 findBook(Integer id) {
    System.out.println("Finding NAME_1 with id: " + id);
    // Pretend this is a DB call
    return new NAME_1(id, "Some NAME_1 title");
}

@CachePut("books")
public NAME_1 updateBook(NAME_1 NAME_1) {
    System.out.println("Updating NAME_1: " + NAME_1);
    // Pretend this updates a DB
    return NAME_1;
}

@CacheEvict("books")
public void deleteBook(Integer id) {
    System.out.println("Deleting NAME_1 with id: " + id);
    // Pretend this deletes from a DB
}
```

We have:

- @Cacheable("books") on findBook() to cache its results
- @CachePut("books") on updateBook() to update the cache
- @CacheEvict("books") on deleteBook() to evict from the cache

