Here is an example of a Kotlin extension function that simplifies using JOOQ transactions:

```kotlin
import org.jooq.ExecuteListener
import org.jooq.Transaction
import org.jooq.impl.DSL
import org.jooq.lambda.Unchecked

fun DSL.beginTransaction(): Transaction {
    val tx = Unchecked.get()
    tx.setStatus(org.jooq.Transaction.Status.STARTED)
    return tx
}

fun DSL.commitTransaction(): Transaction {
    val tx = Unchecked.get()
    tx.setStatus(org.jooq.Transaction.Status.COMMITTED)
    return tx
}

fun DSL.rollbackTransaction(): Transaction {
    val tx = Unchecked.get()
    tx.setStatus(org.jooq.Transaction.Status.ROLLED_BACK)
    return tx
}

fun DSL.executeWithTransaction(listener: ExecuteListener<Any>) {
    val tx = this.beginTransaction()
    try {
        listener.apply(this)
        tx.commit()
    } catch (ex: Exception) {
        tx.rollback()
        throw ex
    } finally {
        tx.complete()
    }
}
```

This extension function adds four methods to the DSL class: `beginTransaction`, `commitTransaction`, `rollbackTransaction`, and `executeWithTransaction`. The `executeWithTransaction` method takes an `ExecuteListener` as a parameter, which is a callback that will be executed within the transaction. If an exception is thrown during the execution of the listener, the transaction will be rolled back.

Yes, in Spring Boot you can manipulate the response using ResponseFilter. A ResponseFilter allows you to intercept the response before it is written to the client.

For example, you can have a ResponseFilter like this:
```java
@Component
public class MyResponseFilter implements ResponseFilter {
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain) 
            throws IOException, ServletException {
        HttpServletResponse httpResponse = (HttpServletResponse) response;
        httpResponse.addHeader("My-Header", "Some value");
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
} 
```

This will add a "My-Header" header to all responses.

To register this filter, you need to add it to the filters list in your application.properties:

```
spring.servlet.response.filters=MyResponseFilter 
```

Now all responses from your Spring Boot app will have the "My-Header" header added.

You can perform any manipulation on the HttpServletResponse in the doFilter method - add/remove headers, change content type, modify response body, etc.

