EF (Entity Framework) is a framework that enables the persistence of data in a database. It is an open-source framework that allows developers to create database-driven applications by using the Entity Framework.

In EF, you can use the Entity Framework to access and manipulate data in your database. The Entity Framework is built on top of the Relational Database Model (RDBMS) and provides a set of classes and interfaces that make it easy to create data-binding and data access.

Here is an example of how you can use Entity Framework in a C# ASP.NET project:
```csharp
using System.Data.Entity;
using System.Linq;
using System.Web;

public class Person
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

public class PersonRepository : Repository<Person>
{
    public Person GetById(int id)
    {
        Person person = entity.Find(id);
        return person;
    }
}

public class PersonController : Controller
{
    private PersonRepository _repository;

    public PersonController(PersonRepository repository)
    {
        _repository = repository;
    }

    // GET: Person
    [HttpGet]
    public ActionResult GetPerson()
    {
        return View();
    }

    // GET: Person/Create
    [HttpPost]
    public ActionResult CreatePerson(Person person)
    {
        _repository.Add(person);
        _repository.SaveChanges();

        return RedirectToAction("Index");
    }

    // POST: Person
    [HttpPost]
    public ActionResult CreatePerson(Person person)
    {
        if (!ModelState.IsValid)
        {
            return View(person);
        }

        _repository.SaveChanges();

        return RedirectToAction("Index");
    }
}
```
This is an example of how you can use Entity Framework in a C# ASP.NET project to create a database-driven application. You can also use LINQ to SQL and Entity Framework to access and manipulate data in a database.

