In React, error boundaries, also known as error handling components, are components that wrap around other components and handle any errors that may occur during rendering. These components are used to prevent the application from crashing and to provide a better user experience by displaying informative error messages.

Error boundaries are typically implemented using the `try-catch` statement or a custom error-handling function. When an error occurs during rendering, the error boundary component will catch the error and render a fallback component or a message to the user.

For example, you could create a custom error boundary component called `WithErrors` that takes an element and a fallback message as props:
```kotlin
import React from 'react';

function WithErrors(props) {
  const { element, fallbackMessage } = props;

  try {
    return element;
  } catch (error) {
    return <div>{fallbackMessage}</div>;
  }
}

export default WithErrors;
```
Then, you can use this error boundary component to wrap any component that may throw an error:
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import WithErrors from './WithErrors';

function NAME_1() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {
      setCount(count + 1);
    }, 1000);

    return () => clearInterval(intervalId);
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <WithErrors>
      <div>
        <h1>Count</h1>
        <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      </div>
    </WithErrors>
  );
}

export default NAME_1;
```
In this example, the `WithErrors` component is used to wrap the `h1` and `p` elements. If any error occurs during rendering, the error will be caught by the `WithErrors` component and the fallback message will be displayed instead of the original content.

`useEffect` is a hook in React that lets you synchronize a component with an external system, or perform some side effects, such as updating the document title, making a network request, or sending an analytics event.

The `useEffect` hook takes two arguments: a function that contains the code to be executed, and an array of dependencies. The function will be executed after the component has rendered, and the array of dependencies specifies which variables should trigger a re-render of the component.

Here's an example of how you might use `useEffect` to fetch data from an API and update the component's state:
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function Example() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1')
      .then((response) => response.json())
      .then((data) => setData(data));
  }, []); // empty dependency array

  return data === null ? <p>Loading...</p> : <p>Data: {data.title}</p>;
}

export default Example;
```
In this example, the `useEffect` hook is used to fetch data from the `jsonplaceholder` API when the component mounts. The empty dependency array (`[]`) means that the effect will only be run once, when the component mounts.

The first `then` callback is passed the response object, and the second `then` callback is passed the data as a JavaScript object. Both of these callbacks call the `setData` function to update the component's state with the fetched data.

Finally, the component checks if the `data` variable is `null`, and if so, it renders a loading message. Otherwise, it renders the `title` property of the `data` object.

Note that you should always ensure that the effect is idempotent, meaning that it can be called multiple times without changing the result. In this example, the `fetch` function is idempotent because it returns a cached response if the same URL has been requested before.

