Here is C# code to calculate points on an ellipse:
```csharp
public class Ellipse 
{
    public double X { get; set; }
    public double Y { get; set; }
    public double A { get; set; }
    public double B { get; set; }

    public List<Point> GetPoints(int numPoints = 100)
    {
        List<Point> points = new List<Point>();
        double step = 2 * Math.PI / numPoints;
        for (double theta = 0; theta < 2 * Math.PI; theta += step) 
        {
            double x = X + A * Math.Cos(theta);
            double y = Y + B * Math.Sin(theta);
            points.Add(new Point(x, y));
        }
        return points;
    }
}
```

This Ellipse class has properties for the center point (X, Y) and radii (A, B). 
The GetPoints method calculates numPoints points on the ellipse perimeter by stepping through angles from 0 to 2PI. 
For each angle, it calculates the corresponding point on the ellipse using the ellipse equation:

x = X + A * cos(theta)
y = Y + B * sin(theta)

It adds these points to the list which is returned. So you can do:

```csharp
Ellipse ellipse = new Ellipse { X = 0, Y = 0, A = 10, B = 5 };
List<Point> points = ellipse.GetPoints();
```

And `points` will contain 100 points approximating the ellipse perimeter.

