Sure, here's an example of a basic HTML simulation of the two-body problem:
```php
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Two-Body Problem</title>
    <style>
      canvas {
        border: 1px solid black;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <canvas id="canvas" width="500" height="500"></canvas>
    <script>
      const canvas = document.getElementById("canvas");
      const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
      let x = 0;
      let y = 0;
      let dx = 2;
      let dy = 2;
      let distance = 50;
      let mass = 1;
      let gravitationalConstant = 0.002247;

      function draw() {
        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
        ctx.beginPath();
        ctx.moveTo(x, y);
        ctx.lineTo(x + dx, y + dy);
        ctx.stroke();
        x += dx;
        y += dy;
      }

      function simulate() {
        draw();
        let forceX = gravitationalConstant * (mass / (distance * distance));
        let forceY = gravitationalConstant * (mass / (distance * distance));
        dx = forceX;
        dy = forceY;
        distance -= 0.1;
        if (distance < 5) {
          dx *= -1;
          dy *= -1;
        }
      }

      setInterval(simulate, 10);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```
This code uses the `canvas` and `context` APIs to draw a simple two-body simulation on a webpage. It updates the position of the two bodies based on the gravitational force between them, simulating their motion over time. You can modify the code to customize the appearance and behavior of the simulation to suit your needs.

