If you're writing a library in C#, you can use the built-in Exception class to convey an exception that contains an error code. You can create a custom exception class that derives from the Exception class and add any additional properties or methods that you need for your specific use case.

Here's an example of how you can create a custom exception class that contains an error code:
```csharp
public class MyException : Exception
{
    public int ErrorCode { get; set; }

    public MyException(string message, int errorCode) : base(message)
    {
        ErrorCode = errorCode;
    }
}
```
You can then use this custom exception class to throw an exception with an error code, like this:
```csharp
try
{
    // code that can throw an exception
}
catch (MyException ex)
{
    // handle the exception
    Console.WriteLine("Error code: {0}", ex.ErrorCode);
}
```
Using a custom exception class can be useful if you need to add additional context or metadata to the exception that isn't provided by the built-in Exception class. However, if you only need to convey an error code and don't need to add any additional metadata, using the built-in Exception class is a simpler and more straightforward approach.

