In C, header files are used to declare the functions, variables, and other entities that make up the public interface of a program or library. A header file typically contains a set of #defines, #includes, and function declarations that can be used by multiple source files in a program or library.

Here's an example of a simple header file called "myheader.h":
```c
#ifndef MYHEADER_H
#define MYHEADER_H

int myFunction(int arg1, int arg2);

#endif /* MYHEADER_H */
```
This header file contains a single function declaration:
```c
int myFunction(int arg1, int arg2);
```
The function declaration includes a return type (int), two parameters (arg1 and arg2, both of type int), and a function name (myFunction).

Here's an example of a source file that includes the header file:
```c
#include "myheader.h"

int main() {
    int result = myFunction(1, 2);
    return 0;
}
```
In this source file, the #include statement tells the compiler to include the contents of the myheader.h file. The result of the function call (myFunction(1, 2)) is assigned to the variable "result", and the value of "result" is returned as the exit status of the program.

By using header files to define the public interface of a program or library, you can encapsulate the implementation details of that interface and prevent other parts of the program from directly accessing or modifying those details. This makes it easier to modify and reuse code, as well as to maintain the overall structure and organization of a program or library.

