3. The method catches FormatException but then silently returns 0. It should re-throw the exception instead.

4. The method does not have null checking - it will throw a NullReferenceException if obj is null.

Here is a revised version that fixes these issues:

```csharp
public static double TryParseDouble(object obj) 
{
    if (obj == null) {
        throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(obj));
    }

    if (obj is JSONNode) 
    {
        if (double.TryParse(obj.ToString(), NumberStyles.AllowDecimalPoint, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var v)) 
        {
            return v;
        }
        throw new FormatException("Unable to parse JSONNode as double");
    }

    try {
        return Convert.ToDouble(obj);
    } catch (FormatException ex) {
        throw new FormatException("Unable to parse object as double", ex);
    }
}
```

Revised method:

- Is named TryParseDouble to indicate it can fail 
- Throws FormatException instead of returning 0 for JSONNode
- Re-throws caught FormatException instead of returning 0 
- Adds null checking

