That express.errorHandler middleware configuration is not recommended. It will display sensitive error details to end users, which is a security risk.

It's better to use:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  res.status(err.status || 500);
  res.json({
    error: {
      message: err.message
    }
  });
});
```

This will send a generic 500 Internal Server Error response to the client, without exposing any sensitive error details.

You can then log the full error details to your server logs for debugging, using a logger like:

```js
const logger = require('./logger');

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  logger.error(err.stack);
  // Rest of code...
});
```

So in summary:

- Don't expose error details to end users
- Send generic responses to end users
- Log full error details to your server logs for debugging 

This follows security best practices for error handling in Express. Let me know if you have any other questions!

