Server-sent events (SSEs) are a way for web pages to receive real-time updates from a server without reloading the page. SSEs use the `` EventSource `` API to establish a connection between the page and the server. When the server wants to send an update, it sends a message to the `` EventSource `` API using the `` addEventListener() `` method. The `` EventSource `` API then sends the message to all connected pages that have subscribed to the event.

To use SSEs, you need to add the `` <script> `` tag with the appropriate `` src `` attribute to your HTML page. For example:


```html
<script src="sse.js"></script>

```

In the JavaScript file, you need to add the code to establish a connection between the page and the server:


```javascript
const sse = new EventSource('/sse');

```

This line creates an `` EventSource `` object that is connected to the `` /sse `` endpoint on the server.

To receive updates from the server, you can use the `` addEventListener() `` method:


```javascript
sse.addEventListener('data', (event) => {
  // handle incoming data
});

```

This line adds an `` event listener `` to the `` data `` event, which is triggered when the server sends an update.

You can also use the `` error() `` and `` closed `` event listeners to handle errors and disconnections:


```javascript
sse.addEventListener('error', (event) => {
  // handle errors
});

sse.addEventListener('closed', () => {
  // handle disconnections
});

```

SSEs are a useful way to handle real-time updates on a web page without having to refresh the page. They can be used for things like chat applications, stock tickers, and more.

