Here's an example of a bump allocator in C++. A bump allocator is a memory allocation strategy that guarantees that memory is always allocated in a contiguous block, allowing for efficient processing of large amounts of data.
```c
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>

class BumpAllocator {
public:
    void* allocate(size_t size) {
        void* ptr = malloc(size);
        if (ptr == NULL) {
            throw std::bad_alloc();
        }
        return ptr;
    }

    void free(void* ptr, size_t size) {
        free(ptr);
    }
};

int main() {
    BumpAllocator allocator;

    void* ptr1 = allocator.allocate(1024);
    void* ptr2 = allocator.allocate(1024);
    void* ptr3 = allocator.allocate(1024);

    // Use the memory blocks...

    allocator.free(ptr1, 1024);
    allocator.free(ptr2, 1024);
    allocator.free(ptr3, 1024);

    return 0;
}
```
This is a very basic example, but it should give you an idea of how a bump allocator works. The `allocate` method allocates memory of the specified size using the `malloc` function, and returns a pointer to the allocated memory. The `free` method frees the memory using the `free` function.

One thing to note is that the `malloc` and `free` functions are part of the C standard library and are not part of the C++ standard library. This means that you will need to include the `cstdlib` header in order to use them.

Also, in a real-world application, you would want to add additional error checking and logging to the `allocate` and `free` methods to make sure that memory allocation and deallocation are always successful, and to keep track of the amount of memory allocated and deallocated.

