RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) is a pattern for managing resources in C++ that allows for automatic memory management. It ensures that resources are acquired and released in the correct order and that resources are properly cleaned up when they are no longer needed.

Here's an example of how RAII works:


```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <memory>

class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() : data(new String("Hello, World!")) {}

    MyClass(const std::string& str) : data(str) {}

    MyClass(const MyClass& other) : data(other.data->clone()) {}

    ~MyClass() { delete data; }

    const std::string& getData() const { return data->c_str(); }

private:
    std::string* data;
};

int main() {
    MyClass obj1("Hello, World!");
    MyClass obj2(obj1);
    MyClass obj3(obj2);

    std::cout << obj1.getData() << std::endl; // prints "Hello, World!"
    std::cout << obj2.getData() << std::endl; // prints "Hello, World!"
    std::cout << obj3.getData() << std::endl; // prints "Hello, World!"

    return 0;
}

```

In this example, the `` MyClass `` class has a `` data `` member that points to a dynamically-allocated string. When an instance of `` MyClass `` is created, the `` data `` member is initialized with a new `` String `` object.

When an instance of `` MyClass `` is destroyed, the `` delete `` operator is used to release the memory occupied by the `` String `` object. The `` clone `` method is used to create a new `` String `` object with the same content as the original string, which is then stored in the `` data `` member of the new `` MyClass `` instance.

By using RAII, the `` MyClass `` class ensures that resources are acquired and released in the correct order and that resources are properly cleaned up when they are no longer needed.

