To force one div to be physically above the next on screen using CSS, you can use the `` z-index `` property. The `` z-index `` property is used to specify the stacking order of elements on the page. Elements with a higher `` z-index `` will be displayed on top of elements with a lower `` z-index ``.

Here is an example of how you can use `` z-index `` to force one div to be physically above the next on screen:


```css#div1 {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  z-index: 1;
}

#div2 {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  z-index: 0;
}
```

In this example, the `` #div1 `` has the `` z-index `` of 1, which means it will be displayed on top of `` #div2 `` which has the `` z-index `` of 0.

Another way to achieve this is by using `` position:relative `` property on the div that you want to be above and `` position:absolute `` on the div you want to be below and then use the `` top `` and `` left `` properties to position the divs where you want them to be.

Please keep in mind that this is just one way to force one div to be physically above the next on screen and there are other ways to achieve this as well.

