To detect if the numbers in the array are decreasing, you can compare the adjacent elements in the array. Here's an example code snippet in Kotlin that implements this logic:
```kotlin
fun main() {
    val numbers = arrayOf(0.2f, 0.3f, 0.2f, 0.1f)

    var i = 0
    while (i < numbers.size - 1) {
        if (numbers[i + 1] < numbers[i]) {
            println("Decreasing sequence detected from index $i to index ${i + 1}")
            break
        }
        i++
    }
}
```
In this code, we start at the first element of the array and compare it with the next element. If the next element is less than the current element, we know that the numbers are decreasing and print out a message indicating the starting and ending index of the decreasing sequence. We then move on to the next pair of adjacent elements and repeat the comparison. The loop continues until we reach the end of the array.

This implementation assumes that the array has at least two elements. If the array has only one element, it will not contain a decreasing sequence.

