If you want to load a C++ shared library dynamically at runtime, you will need to use a programming language that supports dynamic loading of libraries, such as C++ or Python.

Here is an example of how you might load a C++ shared library dynamically in C++:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <dlfcn.h>  // for dlopen, dlsym, dlclose

int main()
{
    void* handle = dlopen("libmylib.so", RTLD_LAZY);  // load the library
    if (!handle)
    {
        std::cerr << "Error loading library: " << dlerror() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Get the address of the "mylib_func" function
    int (*mylib_func)(int, const char*) = (int (*)(int, const char*)) dlsym(handle, "mylib_func");
    if (!mylib_func)
    {
        std::cerr << "Error getting function address: " << dlerror() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Call the function
    int result = mylib_func(123, "Hello, world!");
    if (result != 456)
    {
        std::cerr << "Error: mylib_func returned " << result << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    dlclose(handle);  // close the library handle

    return 0;
}
```
This code uses the `dlopen`, `dlsym`, and `dlclose` functions from the `<dlfcn.h>` header to dynamically load the "libmylib.so" shared library and access its functions.

You will need to replace "libmylib.so" with the actual name of your shared library. You will also need to replace "mylib\_func" with the actual name of the function you want to call.

