Using `fetch`:
```javascript
fetch('https://example.com/q/3454353?', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    ok: true,
    score: "2"
  })
})
.then(response => response.json())
.then(data => console.log(data))
.catch(error => console.error(error))
```
Using `XMLHttpRequest`:
```javascript
const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('POST', 'https://example.com/q/3454353?');
xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
xhr.send(JSON.stringify({
  ok: true,
  score: "2"
}));
xhr.onload = function() {
  if (xhr.status === 200) {
    console.log(JSON.parse(xhr.responseText));
  } else {
    console.error('Request failed.  Returned status of ' + xhr.status);
  }
};
```
In both examples, the `fetch` or `XMLHttpRequest` function is used to make a request to the specified URL with a `POST` method and a `Content-Type` header set to `application/json`. The request body contains a JSON object with the `ok` and `score` properties.

The response from the server is handled in the `then` or `onload` callback function, where you can access the response data and handle it as needed.

