Here is an example of a quicksort function in C++:
```c
void quicksort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
  if (low < high) {
    int pivot = partition(arr, low, high);
    quicksort(arr, low, pivot - 1);
    quicksort(arr, pivot + 1, high);
  }
}

int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
  int pivot = arr[high];
  int i = low - 1;
  for (int j = low; j <= high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
    }
  }
  swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high]);
  return i + 1;
}
```
This quicksort function takes an array of integers, `arr`, and the indices of the left and right endpoints of the array, `low` and `high`. It then partitions the array using the `partition()` function, which determines the index of the pivot element (the element that separates the smaller elements from the larger ones) and swaps the pivot element with the last element in the array. Finally, the `quicksort()` function recursively sorts the elements to the left and right of the pivot element.

Note that this is a basic implementation of quicksort and there are many variations and optimizations that can be made.

