```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;

public class TaskCounter
{
    private int _taskCount;
    private CountdownEvent _countdownEvent;

    public TaskCounter(int initialTaskCount)
    {
        _taskCount = initialTaskCount;
        _countdownEvent = new CountdownEvent(initialTaskCount);
    }

    public void IncrementTaskCount()
    {
        Interlocked.Increment(ref _taskCount);
    }

    public void DecrementTaskCount()
    {
        Interlocked.Decrement(ref _taskCount);
        _countdownEvent.Signal();
    }

    public void WaitUntilZero()
    {
        _countdownEvent.Wait();
    }
}
```
The `TaskCounter` class has a `CountdownEvent` that's initialized with the number of initial tasks. When a new task starts, you call `IncrementTaskCount()`, and when a task finishes, you call `DecrementTaskCount()`. The `WaitUntilZero()` method will block until the task count reaches zero.

Here's an example of how to use this class:
```csharp
class Program
{
