To pause and resume a thread using `pthread`, you can use the `pthread_yield()` function to yield the current thread to the scheduler. When the scheduler picks up the thread again, it will resume execution from where it left off. To pause the thread, you can simply call `pthread_yield()` to give control back to the scheduler. To resume the thread, you can call `pthread_resume()` with the same argument that you passed to `pthread_yield()`.

Here's an example of how to use `pthread_yield()` and `pthread_resume()` to pause and resume a thread:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

void *my_thread_function(void *arg) {
  // This is the thread function
  while (1) {
    // Do some work
    // ...

    // Pause the thread
    pthread_yield();

    // Resume the thread
    pthread_resume();
  }
  return NULL;
}

int main() {
  // Create the thread
  pthread_t thread_id;
  pthread_create(&thread_id, NULL, my_thread_function, NULL);

  // Wait for the thread to finish
  pthread_join(thread_id, NULL);

  return 0;
}
```
In this example, the thread function performs some work and then pauses the thread using `pthread_yield()`. After the thread is resumed, it continues executing until it finishes.

