Yes, you can access JavaScript objects and variables in the browser console. Here's how:

1. Open the developer tools in your browser (CTRL+SHIFT+I in Chrome, CTRL+ALT+I in Firefox).

2. Go to the "Console" tab. This will open the interactive JS console.

3. Type the name of any global variable or object defined in your JS files. Press Enter.

4. The value of that variable/object will be displayed. You can then call methods on objects, reassign properties, etc.

For example, say you have this in your JS code:
```js
const myObj = {
  name: "NAME_4",
  age: 30,
  sayHello: function() {
    console.log("Hello!"); 
  }
};
```

In your browser console, you can do:

```js
myObj;
// {name: "NAME_4", age: 30, sayHello: ƒ}

myObj.name;
// "NAME_4"

myObj.sayHello();
// "Hello!"

myObj.age = 31; 
// 31
```

So the console gives you an interactive "REPL" environment to access your JS state and manipulate objects. This can be super useful for debugging, testing snippets of code, and checking on the state of your application.

A few other tips:

- Use $0, $1, $2, etc to access the most recently evaluated expressions.
- Type "clear()" to clear the console. 
- You can create new variables and objects that will persist in your JS environment.
- Use the Up/Down arrow keys to navigate your console history.

