In C++, the `->` operator is used to access members of an object through a pointer to a member. It is used to differentiate between a pointer to an object and a pointer to a member of an object.

The `->` operator is read as "dot-and-arrow" and can be read as "get the value of the member of the object that this pointer points to." Here's an example:
```c
class MyClass {
public:
    int x;
};

int main() {
    MyClass obj;
    obj.x = 10;

    // Access the member 'x' using the '->' operator
    MyClass* p = &obj;
    int y = p->x;  // y equals 10

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, `p` is a pointer to an object of the `MyClass` class. The `->` operator is used to access the `x` member of the object that `p` points to, and the value of `x` is stored in the `y` variable.

