Here is the bubble sort algorithm implemented in JavaScript:

```js
const bubbleSort = (array) => {
  let swapped = true;

  while (swapped) {
    swapped = false;

    for (let i = 0; i < array.length - 1; i++) {
      if (array[i] > array[i + 1]) {
        [array[i], array[i + 1]] = [array[i + 1], array[i]];
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
  }

  return array;
};
```

This works by iterating over the array multiple times. On each iteration, it checks if any two adjacent elements are in the wrong order. If so, it swaps them. This process is repeated until no swaps are needed, meaning the array is sorted.

The code keeps track of whether any swaps were made using the `swapped` variable. If no swaps were made in an iteration, the array must be sorted and the function exits.

You can test the function like this:
```js
const result = bubbleSort([5, 3, 2, 1, 4]); 
console.log(result); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

The time complexity of bubble sort is O(n2) since we have nested loops. It is not the most efficient sorting algorithm, but it is simple to understand and implement.

