The keywords const, let, and var are used to declare variables in JavaScript. Here are the main differences between them:

const:

- Declares a constant variable whose value cannot be changed.
- Must be initialized to a value when declared.
- Cannot be re-declared.

let: 

- Declares a block-scoped variable whose value can be changed.
- Must be initialized to a value when declared.
- Cannot be re-declared in the same scope.

var:

- Declares a function-scoped variable whose value can be changed. 
- Can be declared without initializing a value. 
- Can be re-declared in the same scope.

So in summary:

Use const when you want to declare a constant whose value will not change.

Use let when you want to declare a variable with block scope.

Use var when you want to declare a variable with function scope.

Some examples:

```js
// const
const PI = 3.14; 

// let
let x = 1; 
x = 2; 

// var
var y = 1;
var y = 2; // Okay, re-declaration allowed
```

```js
// Block scope
if (true) {
  let x = 1;  // x only exists in this block
}

// Function scope
function foo() {
  var x = 1;   // x only exists in this function
}
```

Hope this helps clarify the difference between const, let, and var in JavaScript! Let me know if you have any other questions.

