Here is an example code to sort an array of integers in ascending order using the "bubble sort" algorithm:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, temp;
    for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 2, 1, 5, 3};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubbleSort(arr, n);
    printf("Sorted array: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```
This code takes an array of integers and the number of elements in the array as input. It uses a nested loop to compare adjacent elements in the array and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The loop continues until the array is sorted. The `main()` function simply calls the `bubbleSort()` function with the input array and prints the sorted array to the console.

Note that there are many other sorting algorithms that are more efficient than bubble sort for large arrays of data.

