In JavaScript, closure is a function that refers to itself with the same name as the outer function. When a function is defined within another function, it is a closure.


```javascript
let outer = function () {
  let inner = function () {
    console.log("Hello from inner function");
  };
  inner();
  return 5;
};

let result = outer(); // prints "Hello from inner function" to the console

```

When a function is defined within another function, the inner function can reference the outer function using the same name. The outer function will be closed over by the inner function, meaning that any changes made to the outer function's variables will be reflected in the inner function.

Closures are often used to encapsulate variables and functions, making them more difficult to access or modify from outside the closure. They can also be used to create functions that can be passed as arguments to other functions, without exposing the variables and functions of the outer function.

