  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    result += i;
  }
  return result;
};

let counter2 = (n) => {
  let result = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    result += i;
  }
  return result;
};

console.log(counter(10)); // prints 50
console.log(counter2(10)); // prints 50

```

In this example, both `` counter `` and `` counter2 `` are defined as functions that take a number as an argument and return the sum of the numbers up to that number. However, `` counter `` is a closure because it references the outer function `` counter2 `` using the same name.

When `` counter `` is called with `` 10 `` as an argument, it will create an inner function `` i `` that references the outer function `` counter2 ``. The inner function will use the outer function to calculate the sum of the numbers up to `` 10 ``, and then return the result. This means that any changes made to the outer function `` counter2 `` will be reflected in the inner function `` counter ``, making it more difficult to access or modify the outer function from outside the closure.

On the other hand, `` counter2 `` is not a closure because it does not reference the outer function `` counter ``. It is a regular function that is defined separately from `` counter ``. When `` counter2 `` is called with `` 10 `` as an argument, it will calculate the sum of the numbers up to `` 10 `` using a different set of variables than the `` i `` variable used by `` counter ``. This means that any changes made to the outer function `` counter `` will not affect the inner function `` counter2 ``, making it more difficult to access or modify the outer function from outside the closure.

