Sure, here's an implementation of the bubble sort algorithm in PHP that uses foreach:
```php
function bubbleSort($array) {
    // Traverse the array until it's sorted
    $n = count($array);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
        // Last i elements are already in place
        foreach (range(0, $i) as $j) {
            // If the current element is greater than the next element, swap them
            if ($array[$j] > $array[$j+1]) {
                swap($array, $j, $j+1);
            }
        }
    }
    return $array;
}
```
This implementation of bubble sort uses a foreach loop to iterate over the array. Within the loop, another foreach loop is used to iterate over the first i elements, which are assumed to be already in place. Then, for each iteration of the inner foreach loop, if the current element is greater than the next element, the two elements are swapped. This process is repeated until the entire array is sorted.

This implementation is compact, smart, and readable for humans because it uses a foreach loop, which is a concise and intuitive way to iterate over arrays, and it also makes use of the range() function to iterate over a specific range of elements. Furthermore, the algorithm itself is simple and easy to understand, and the code is well-structured and organized.

