Here's an example of a tokenizer for.log files in TypeScript for Monaco Editor:

1. Import necessary modules:
```typescript
import { MonacoEditor, ITokenizer } from "monaco-editor";
import * as fs from "fs";
```
2. Define a function to tokenize the log file:
```typescript
function tokenizeLogFile(filePath: string): Promise<string[]> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fs.readFile(filePath, (err, data) => {
      if (err) {
        reject(err);
      } else {
        let tokens: string[] = [];
        let line = "";
        let lastIndex = 0;
        for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
          line = data.substring(i, i + 1);
          if (line.endsWith(".log")) {
            tokens = tokens.concat(line.split(" "));
          } else {
            tokens = tokens.concat(line.split("\n"));
            lastIndex = i;
          }
        }
        resolve(tokens.slice(lastIndex));
      }
    });
  });
}
```
3. Define an adapter to tokenize the log file for Monaco Editor:
```typescript
function logFileAdapter(editor: MonacoEditor, filePath: string): Promise<string[]> {
  return tokenizeLogFile(filePath).then(tokens => {
    tokens.forEach((token, index) => {
      if (index % 2 === 1) {
        editor.insertText(token);
      }
    });
  });
}
```
4. Use the adapter in your Monaco Editor instance:
```typescript
const editor = new MonacoEditor();
editor.tokenizedLanguages.add("log", logFileAdapter);
```
This code defines an adapter that uses the `tokenizeLogFile` function to tokenize the log file and inserts the tokens into the editor. The `logFileAdapter` function is passed to the `tokenizedLanguages` property of the Monaco Editor instance, so that it can be used to tokenize log files.

