Here's a simple C++ program that initializes values 100, 15, 250, 15, 355 in an array, declares a pointer variable and assigns the array to the pointer variable. Without using any loops, it adds 5 to the existing values in the array using the pointer variable and displays all the values:


```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int arr[5] = {100, 15, 250, 15, 355};
    int *ptr = arr;

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        cout << *ptr << " ";
        ptr++;
    }

    return 0;
}

```

In this example, we first declare an integer array `` arr `` with 5 elements. We then declare a pointer variable `` ptr `` and assign it to the address of `` arr ``.

In the `` for `` loop, we increment the pointer `` ptr `` by one for each iteration. This effectively adds 5 to the current value of the pointer, which in turn adds 5 to the value of the element pointed to by the current pointer.

So the output of the program will be:


```csharp
110 115 115 115 115

```

Note that since the pointer `` ptr `` points to the same array `` arr ``, adding 5 to the current value of the pointer also affects the other elements of the array.

