  console.log("Both conditions are true");
} else {
  console.log("One or both conditions are false");
}
```
In this example, the `if` statement checks if `x` is less than 10 and `y` is greater than 5. If both conditions are true, the code inside the `if` block will execute and the message "Both conditions are true" will be logged to the console. If either of the conditions is false, the code inside the `else` block will execute and the message "One or both conditions are false" will be logged to the console.

Note that the `&&` operator has higher precedence than most other operators in JavaScript, so you don't need to use parentheses to group the conditions. However, if you have multiple conditions and some of them have higher precedence than others (for example, because of operator chaining), you may need to use parentheses to ensure that the conditions are evaluated in the correct order.

