In a Compose application, navigation functions in a similar way to how they do in a regular React application. Compose applications are built on top of React, so the core navigation and routing logic is still implemented using React's `` Routes `` component.

Here's an example of how you can define a navigation route in a Compose application:


```javascript
import { Route } from 'react-router-dom';
import { AppContainer } from '@compost/react';

const NAME_1 = () => {
  return (
    <AppContainer>
      <Route path="/" component={HomePage} />
    </AppContainer>
  );
};

export default NAME_1;

```

In this example, the `` NAME_1 `` component returns an `` AppContainer `` component from the `` @compost/react `` library. The `` AppContainer `` component wraps the entire application and provides access to the global state of the application (such as the component that rendered the navigation route).

The `` Route `` component defines the navigation route, and the `` component `` prop is used to specify the component that should be rendered when the user navigates to that route.

You can also use the `` useHistory `` hook from the `` react-router-dom `` library to manage the navigation state of the application. This hook provides methods to add, remove, and update navigation history.


```javascript
import { useHistory } from 'react-router-dom';

const HomePage = () => {
  const history = useHistory();
  history.push('/about');
};

export default HomePage;

```

In this example, the `` HomePage `` component uses the `` useHistory `` hook to navigate to the `` /about `` route when the page is rendered. The `` push `` method is used to add a new route to the navigation history.

Overall, navigation in a Compose application follows the same patterns and principles as in a regular React application, with the added benefit of being able to access the global state of the application through the `` AppContainer `` component.

